  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_left]` is correct. It is a fenced citation with commentary on an explicit function, which is a permitted form.\n\n- **Substantive reason:** the function is `Ok(())`. It exists only because `indicatif::TermLike` requires it, and `RecordingTerm` is compiled only under `#[cfg(test)]`. It contains no application logic, so there is nothing for a test to verify. This is a reason, not just difficulty.\n- **Agrees with the source:** the fixed-width display tests (`stage_and_conversion_counts_are_visible_then_cleared`, `preparation_activity_keeps_updating_without_a_stage_change`) draw through `RecordingTerm`. None of them requests a horizontal move. The adapter's own `write_str`, `clear_line` and `move_cursor_up` are the paths they use.\n- **PTY claim:** the commentary says real terminal movement is exercised through the PTY integration test. `tests/execution_coverage.rs#fn[coverage_progress_on_a_pty_preserves_reports_and_plain_output_policy]` runs coverage progress on a real `openpty` terminal. It asserts that escape sequences update the display in place, and that the stage is cleared with `\\x1b[2K` on early failure. That supports the claim at the level it is made. It does not show that left movement in particular is exercised, and the commentary does not say so.\n- **Other rules:** the item carries no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the same target, so the citation is not redundant.\n\nI found no gap.",
